};
---
<Docs frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p class="version-note"><strong>Development walkthrough.</strong> These examples follow the CLI development source, including the status presentation in <a href="https://github.com/lcorneliussen/wtc-cli/pull/336">PR #336</a>. Older installed releases can use different spacing and symbols. The other handbook pages retain their labeled release baselines.</p>
  <p>A status row is several observations placed next to one another. Local changes, a green PR check, a clean merge prediction, a reviewer’s approval and a production build answer different questions. Read them together to understand what can happen next.</p>
  <nav class="status-contents" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#read-in-layers">Read in layers</a><a href="#walkthrough">Try the situations</a><a href="#columns">Columns and numbers</a><a href="#support">What we detect</a><a href="#freshness">Freshness and missing facts</a><a href="#controls">Use the live view</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="read-in-layers">Read from checkout to delivery</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Which checkout?</strong> REPO and BRANCH identify the local worktree. <code>⌂ main</code> is detached at the development ref; a named feature branch is active work.</li>
    <li><strong>What differs locally?</strong> <code>±</code> describes working-tree changes or an operation. <code>↑</code> and <code>↓</code> count commits against comparison refs.</li>
    <li><strong>What does the PR report?</strong> Read its identifier, draft/conflict state, then checks, mergeability and reviews in that fixed order.</li>
    <li><strong>What has built?</strong> TEST and PROD observe builds on configured shared branches. After merge, the PR’s STATE names the build tier it is still waiting on.</li>
    <li><strong>How current is this?</strong> The header, refresh log and raw facts tell you whether you are reading current observations, cached facts or an incomplete result.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The repository table answers “what is checked out here?” The separate PR table also tracks enlisted work that may no longer be checked out. Services and next actions appear as additional sections when there is something to show.</p>

  <h2 id="walkthrough">Try complete situations</h2>
  <p>Choose a situation, then compare wide and compact terminals. These are outputs of the actual renderer, with synthetic facts. Optional columns yield on narrower screens; a hidden signal is not a newly unknown fact.</p>
  <StatusExplorer />
  {hasLocalEvidence && <p><a href={`${base}status-evidence/index.html`}>Inspect the actual native capture files →</a> This local evidence set includes PNG terminal-cell previews, raw native ANSI, source fingerprints and per-frame assertions. Every captured frame is checked against the guide’s replay text.</p>}

  <h2 id="columns">Columns and numbers in context</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Representation</th><th>What you are reading</th><th>Useful distinction</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>REPO / BRANCH</td><td>The worktree directory and checked-out branch, or <code>⌂</code> for detached HEAD.</td><td><code>[gone]</code> is a configured upstream Git reports missing; it is not proof the local work is preserved elsewhere.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>±3</code>, <code>·</code>, <code>⚠</code>, <code>✗</code></td><td>Changed porcelain entries, clean tree, an operation in progress, or unmerged paths.</td><td>These are local Git observations. A PR merge conflict can exist with a clean tree.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>↑2 ↓1</code></td><td>Ahead of the upstream (development-ref fallback); behind the development tip or open PR’s base.</td><td>Commit counts can use different refs. They are not file counts or a general “needs push” verdict. JSON names <code>ahead_ref</code> and <code>ahead_basis</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>✗3</code> / <code>✗</code> in ↓</td><td>A local or PR conflict is present; a nonzero behind count is retained when available.</td><td>A PR conflict can mark this cell even when the local ± column is clean.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>#48</code> beside C/M/R</td><td>A PR identifier, followed by checks, mergeability and review facts.</td><td>Numbers are padded across the displayed cohort. Optional link space is reserved when it fits.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CHECKS / MERGE / REVIEWS</td><td>Readable wide headings for C / M / R.</td><td>Each slot keeps its meaning even when another slot is unknown. A failure in one does not erase the others.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>TEST <code>T✓#105</code></td><td>A successful development-tier build/run 105, when development and production map to different branches.</td><td>The heading says TEST; T is its cell prefix. This is separate from the PR’s C result.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PROD <code>P●#53</code></td><td>A pending build/run 53 for the resolved production tier.</td><td>The branch can come from <code>production_ref</code>, <code>release_ref</code> or the mapping below. A build does not independently verify deployment.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>T○#104</code> / <code>P○#52</code></td><td>The build commit is known not to contain this repository row’s HEAD.</td><td>The hollow circle replaces the ordinary result glyph. Open the build or JSON for its underlying result and SHA. Unavailable ancestry stays unknown.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>STATE after merge</td><td><code>→ test</code>, <code>● test</code>, <code>✗ test</code>, <code>? test</code>; the same for prod; then <code>✓ built</code>.</td><td>Waiting for containment, pending, failed, attribution unknown, or followed available builds complete. Plain <code>merged</code> can mean no build follow-up was established.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>LOCAL / SERVICES</td><td>Task marks and the service/tunnel/resource detail table.</td><td>Names and state color belong together. Uppercase default tunnel marks indicate public/unknown exposure, not CI failure.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A normal build-result glyph can still appear when containment is unavailable. Only the hollow circle explicitly establishes that HEAD is outside the build. Inspect <code>head_in_build</code> in JSON to distinguish known containment from an unknown ancestry check.</p>

  <h3>Which branch gets TEST or PROD?</h3>
  <p>The production branch comes from <code>production_ref</code>, then <code>release_ref</code>. Without either, development branch <code>dev</code> uses <code>master</code> for production; other development branches use themselves. An empty <code>default_ref</code> falls back to <code>main</code>. If the branches coincide, only PROD exists. A different production branch also creates a TEST tier for the development branch. Columns still require a reported build and enough terminal width.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Resulting tiers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>default_ref: origin/main</code>, no production/release ref</td><td>PROD: main only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>default_ref: origin/dev</code>, no production/release ref</td><td>TEST: dev; PROD: master</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>default_ref: origin/main</code>, <code>release_ref: origin/release</code></td><td>TEST: main; PROD: release</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>default_ref: origin/main</code>, <code>production_ref: origin/main</code></td><td>PROD: main only</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Read the three PR signals as a sentence</h3>
  <p><code>✓ ↓ ●</code> means “checks passed, behind base, review waiting.” <code>● ✓ ✎</code> means “checks pending, clean mergeability, review commented.” Neither is a complete merge decision.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>Representations</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>C — checks</td><td><code>✓</code> success; <code>✗</code> failure/error; <code>●</code> pending/expected; <code>·</code> absent, unknown or unmapped.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>M — mergeability</td><td><code>✓</code> clean/mergeable; <code>✗</code> conflicting/dirty; <code>↓</code> behind; <code>⊘</code> blocked; <code>·</code> absent, unknown or another state.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>R — reviews</td><td><code>✓</code> approved; <code>✗</code> changes requested; <code>●</code> waiting; <code>✎</code> commented; <code>∅</code> no reviewers; <code>·</code> absent/unknown/none.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><code>◇</code> adds draft state. Wide repository cells place the conflict label and draft marker after the three signal slots. A compact row can replace the long conflict suffix with a leading <code>✗</code> and put draft state before the identifier. In the PR table, conflict takes priority over draft in STATE; the repository cell can retain both markers.</p>
  <p>In the PR table, merged entries no longer associated with their retained branch use dots in C/M/R and move build follow-up into STATE. A retained on-branch entry keeps its recorded C/M/R observations while STATE reports catch-up, extra commits or unknown landing evidence. Those observations are not a new post-merge gate. Build follow-up needs a recorded merge commit; it starts at the PR’s base tier, or the first tier when no base was recorded.</p>

  <h2 id="support">What is detected, and where it comes from</h2>
  <p>This walkthrough covers <strong>local terminal status</strong>. The <a href="../openclaw/">OpenClaw collection panel</a> has its own rendering and gateway-managed workflow; it is not a pixel-for-pixel copy of this table.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Observation</th><th>GitHub</th><th>Bitbucket Cloud / local support</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Checkout and Git differences</td><td colspan="2">Local Git: branch/detachment, upstream state, commit counts, porcelain changes, operations and unmerged paths. No forge is needed for these facts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PR discovery and tracking</td><td>Open PRs for the verified repository/branch, plus collection-local enlisted PRs.</td><td>Open PRs and verified branch matching, plus enlisted PRs. Unsupported origins do not invent a forge association.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PR checks</td><td>The PR’s status-check rollup. Observed failure wins; unfinished or unknown conclusions remain pending. Neutral/skipped conclusions count as settled positive observations.</td><td>Commit statuses and applicable head pipelines, tied to the reported PR head. Incomplete collection cannot claim a clean absence or success.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review summary</td><td><code>reviewDecision</code>, latest reviews, requests and draft state. Comments are distinct from approval.</td><td>Participant approval / changes-requested state. An approval can summarize as approved even when other review work remains; this is not a proof that every reviewer finished.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mergeability</td><td>The forge’s merge state; explicit conflicting mergeability takes precedence.</td><td>Unknown forge mergeability can be supplemented by <code>git merge-tree</code> on the exact reported source/base commits already present locally. No object fetch or actual merge is done for that prediction.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared-branch builds</td><td>Checks on the mapped tier’s branch tip; a linked workflow run number where available.</td><td>Bitbucket Pipelines for mapped branches. A project build hook can replace/refine either provider. Build columns appear only for tiers with reported observations and enough display width.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Build containment / landing</td><td colspan="2">Local ancestry and available forge identities associate builds with HEAD/merge commits. Retained merged branches can report landed, follow-up or unknown, including squash history.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Services and resources</td><td colspan="2">Opt-in local runtime declarations, runner observations, readiness, address/login problems, resource hooks and tunnel exposure. See <a href="../services/">services and previews</a>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The built-in forge adapters target <code>github.com</code> and <code>bitbucket.org</code>. Authentication, configured refs, repository identity and locally available objects determine which observations are possible. R is not the separate <code>wtc review</code> gate: inspect current-head local review trust and hosted reports with <code>wtc review status</code> and <code>wtc review activity</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="freshness">Freshness, missing facts, and compact layouts</h2>
  <p>The live header shows refresh timing and the running CLI version. Active PR details normally use a 90-second forge cache. <code>r</code> requests a refresh, but a fresh screen can still contain cached provider facts. <code>l</code> opens the refresh log for failures and collection progress.</p>
  <p><code>--cached</code> reads the completed snapshot without Git or forge calls. Its age and producer version describe that snapshot. <code>--local</code> previews local facts without a forge refresh; <code>--forge</code> previews Git and forge facts. JSON preserves raw state and optional provenance that the compact table cannot fully explain.</p>
  <p>Normal refreshes can fetch eligible remote refs, record final merge facts, and return eligible clean merged worktrees to the development tip under the existing return policy. Use <code>WTC_STATUS_RETURN_MERGED=off</code> to retain branches for inspection. Local/forge previews and cached reads have their own write boundaries; a normal refresh is more than reading a saved screen.</p>
  <p>A dot can collapse several raw states; for example, GitHub’s other merge states also use <code>·</code>. A blank/hidden column, a clipped <code>…</code>, an absent observation, and a known failure are different situations. Check width, logs and JSON before interpreting a missing symbol as a favorable result.</p>
  <p>Wide tables show readable headings and more build/Git columns. Narrow tables relinquish optional detail, links and signals to preserve the PR identifier and priority state where the available width permits. Use a wider terminal or raw facts for the full representation. Older merged PRs are available through <code>a</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="controls">Use this beside a running local terminal</h2>
  <pre><code>wtc status                 # interactive live view in a terminal
wtc status --no-watch      # one pass
wtc status --json          # raw observations and provenance
wtc status --cached --json # completed snapshot; no refresh
wtc status --local --json  # local preview; no forge calls</code></pre>
  <p>Press <code>?</code> for the in-view explanation; use arrows, Page Up/Down or the wheel to scroll, <code>l</code> for logs and <code>q</code> to quit. Repository names, branches, PR numbers and builds link to their targets when available. Native terminal hyperlinks use your terminal’s modifier-click; ordinary TUI click targets can be disabled with <code>--no-click</code>.</p>
  <p><code>n</code> opens next actions. Treat those as explicit choices: the view refreshes facts before acting. Showing status or browsing this guide does not dispatch an agent prompt. The rows do not replace a merge decision, a review of the exact head, or a deployment check.</p>
  <p>Other sections can show retirement blockers versus unknown coverage and development-tip comparison notes. <code>--all</code> adds collection context; <code>--procs</code> is a separate herdr process view. These modes answer different questions from a single collection’s Git/PR table.</p>
  <details class="source-notes"><summary>Source and scope of this roundup</summary>
    <p>The synthetic examples are generated by <code>scripts/capture-status-guide.mjs</code> from the CLI’s existing recording adapter. That adapter calls the native view and refuses commands. Captures include wide (100), medium (60) and compact (39) column layouts.</p>
    <p>These terminal examples were captured from the renderer in <a href={`https://github.com/lcorneliussen/wtc-cli/tree/${replay.sourceRevision}`}>CLI source {replay.sourceRevision.slice(0, 7)}</a>. Their revision is recorded with the replay data.</p>
    <p>Collection and provider explanations were checked against <a href="https://github.com/lcorneliussen/wtc-cli/tree/5b6a7c29ef8fa4ab9b5761af80b886cc15d47fc8">CLI source 5b6a7c2</a>: status Git/PR collection, forge checks/reviews, build providers and containment, runtime observations, and the existing <a href="https://github.com/lcorneliussen/wtc-cli/blob/5b6a7c29ef8fa4ab9b5761af80b886cc15d47fc8/docs/status-mergeability.md">mergeability</a>, <a href="https://github.com/lcorneliussen/wtc-cli/blob/5b6a7c29ef8fa4ab9b5761af80b886cc15d47fc8/docs/squash-status.md">squash landing</a> and <a href="https://github.com/lcorneliussen/wtc-cli/blob/5b6a7c29ef8fa4ab9b5761af80b886cc15d47fc8/docs/status-version.md">version</a> contracts. This guide changes no collector or readiness policy.</p>
  </details>
